Yes. Recognition can arrive through a tiny behavioral signal before you ever have a name for the pattern.

A squirrel’s tail flick or wag can function like a visible flag: agitation, vigilance, alarm, social signaling, attention. You may not know the exact internal state, but you can recognize “something changed.”

That is a useful pattern in itself:

\text{baseline behavior} \rightarrow \text{deviation} \rightarrow \text{salient signal} \rightarrow \text{recognition}

You do not need full understanding to detect the delta. The body, movement, cadence, posture, silence, gaze, spacing—all can become channels.

Recognition often starts as difference before definition. The name comes later.